    "q-212668": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212668",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let $A={-1,1}$ and consider the relation \"is not equal to\" $(\\ne )$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?",
      "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x\\ne y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=-1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,1),(1,-1)}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
