    "q-212994": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212994",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $A={1,5,8,10}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $6$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.",
      "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(1-1).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=8$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(8-8).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,8).$\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=10$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(10-10).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,10).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(5,5),(8,8),(10,10)}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
