    "q-212992": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212992",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Let $A={2,6,7}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.",
      "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(6-6).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=7$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(7-7).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,7).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(6,6),(7,7)}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
