    "q-213127": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213127",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,3$\n- $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$",
      "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
