    "q-80827": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80827",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Let $X={-3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $X?$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y-2}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $S$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}$ such that $x>y-2.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in X$ to see which elements $y\\in X$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-3,$ then the value $y=-3$ satisfies $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pair $(-3,-3).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=-3,4$ satisfy $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,-3),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS={(-3,-3),(4,-3),(4,4)}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
